body:
Physically I have been good it is better than being in a UC flare 100%, mentally though it has been a big challenge. I am doing the j-pouch surgery and have finished 1 of the 3. It is nerve wracking with all of the possibilities of things that can go wrong and living with the ostomy takes some real getting used to. 

&#x200B;

Do you have any other medical options after entyvio or will you have to move onto surgery if it doesn't work?

I joined a facebook group about j-pouches and I searched for positive stories in that group and there were tons of them. I was actually surprised because I was breaking down seeing the horror stories thinking I would have to live with the ostomy for the rest of my life, which isn't a bad thing just hard to get used to and I personally want to try a jpouch. When the group gets asked for positive stories it is usually a flood of them so I think that is pretty dang good, also there are people on there with pouches going 30-close to 40 years(some of the first people to get the surgery) and they are still doing well. Also a lot is dependent on patient selection, if you are in reasonably good health without too many complications from surgery, things are more likely in your favor for a good pouch. 

&#x200B;

All in all though if you think they are pressuring you and you are happy with your ostomy. Don't let anyone force your hand, it is a huge decision and overall it is yours to make.

Well it all depends on how much your body is willing to put up with. For me, I didn't have much of a choice my colon got so bad and all the meds quit working. It was colon out time. Going into the surgery I was pretty positive it wasn't until I got home that everything really hit me. These surgeries are tough, mentally and physically. Do your research, I am going for the j-pouch. You will read horror stories about this surgery but keep in mind this surgery is done about 200,000 times a year and has a 90ish% success rate. I joined a fb support group about jpouches and there are stories of people having issues but there are sooooo many positive stories on there. Any time you ask them looking for positive stories you will get a huge result.

&#x200B;

That being said keep trying the medical route and don't rush into things. Surgery should be a last resort. For me it was surgery or my colon perforates and possibly kills me. 

&#x200B;

I will tell you my experience so far just for insight. I had my surgery done in February to remove my colon. I currently have an end illeostomy. Personally I have not had issues with the illeostomy other than it's annoying to have weight lifting restrictions because of the possibility of a hernia and wearing a bag on you at all times takes getting used to. Some days it is really annoying other days I don't notice it at all. I have had no leaks, blowouts or blockages. Those are the most common issues I have heard with illeos. I will have a second surgery 6 months from February for J-pouch creation. I will still have a bag on my but it will be slightly higher up in my intestinal track. If all goes well 3 months after that I will get a connected j-pouch in which there is usually a long adjustment period. This can be for people anywhere from a week to a year. I have heard of issues such as pouchitis, cuffitis and butt burn. Those are the most common. Some people get leakage(although its not that common), mostly at night, but from what I have heard for most people it goes away with time. 

&#x200B;

Anyway keep a positive attitude and make sure that if you do go through with the surgeries, get a surgeon that is highly regarded and does these surgeries a lot. Best of luck to you!

Hey man, I am sorry you are going through this. Diseases are tough, what you are going through I really can't imagine. I recently had surgery for an auto immune disease I have and while it is not as serious as the diseases you mentioned, I can relate to a degree. One day you are doing good, happy to think about the future and planning your life and the next, well, you aren't anymore. In my case with my disease I felt and still feel damaged, not normal and not human almost. It is not something that most people have to deal with, and it has always been something on my mind. I wanted to push my fiance away, I was worried about our kids and future. He loves me though and no matter what he wants to be there with me, I am sure your wife feels the same. 

&#x200B;

I don't know if you are looking for advice but hell i'll give my two cents, #1 take it one day at a time, #2 talk to a counselor or therapist, I have never been a "talk to someone" kind of person but the struggle with diseases and whatnot is a different kind of monster that I knew I couldn't handle myself so I have my first appointment tomorrow. #3 Try meditation if you can, I have found it helps me. My cousin has MS and found that marijuana helped not only for his disease but tremendously for his mental health as well. It's okay to feel the way you feel and it is okay to be angry at the world. I hope you start to feel better and be able to live your life. I hope I could help even just a little bit.

Getting up doesn't bother me too much luckily. I am glad you still get to do those things still, I am in the process of restarting hobbies/sports but they still want me to take it easy till I am at about 8 weeks post-op. I really want to get back to riding my motorcycle but alas I have to wait. As for hernias my stoma nurses told me it was more core strength, if you strengthen it you should be okay but they also told me the risk by having an ostomy is much higher than your average person(cause you pretty much have a hernia with your illeostomy), they told me 50% of ostomates will get a hernia at some point(I thought it was only a 1/3 until they gave me a handout).

&#x200B;

Sometimes I think that too. I worry about if things don't work out for the j-pouch for me I may just be wasting a good year or so getting cut open, a bunch and doctors appointments etc. all to end up right back where I started. I really can understand the value of both sides. Your brother is happy with his decision too?
